INT. LAUNDROMAT - NIGHT

Fluorescent tubes buzz over a row of dryers, half of them spinning. Rain streaks the front glass. A vending machine hums in the corner.

BIRGITTA LUNDQVIST (30s, scrubs under a raincoat, tired eyes) feeds quarters into a dryer. Her son TOBIAS (8, pajamas, rain boots on the wrong feet) sits on a folding table swinging his legs and holding a plastic dinosaur. Against the wall, a GIRL (10, school uniform, backpack on both shoulders) sits alone with a library book and does not look up.

                    TOBIAS
          Mom, why is it taking so long?

                    BIRGITTA
          Because it's wet, bud.

                    TOBIAS
          Everything's wet. It's raining.

Birgitta laughs, a short breath of a laugh. She shuts the dryer door and presses the button. The drum begins to turn. Tobias hops down, wanders to the vending machine, and presses his nose to the glass while Birgitta checks her phone, frowns at it, and turns the screen face-down on the table, and the Girl, without lifting her eyes from the page, turns one page over.

                    TOBIAS (CONT'D)
          Can I get the gummy ones?

                    BIRGITTA
          It's ten at night.

                    TOBIAS
          It's eleven. The clock says.

Birgitta looks at the clock. He's right. She sighs and digs in her pocket for coins.

Tobias carries his dinosaur over to the Girl and holds it out, nose-first, a ceremonial offering.

                    TOBIAS (CONT'D)
          This is Gary. He's a stegosaurus.

The Girl looks up. A long, measuring look. Then she takes Gary and studies his plates.

                    GIRL
          Stegosaurs had tiny brains.

                    TOBIAS
          Gary's smart.

                    GIRL
          Then he's not a stegosaurus.

Tobias considers this, deeply wounded, and turns to his mother for ruling. Birgitta crosses over, dropping her coins into the machine on the way, the machine clunking out a bag of gummies, and kneels between the two children.

                    BIRGITTA
          Hey. Where are your parents?

The Girl closes her book on her finger.

                    GIRL
          My dad's coming. He's parking.

                    BIRGITTA
          It's been an hour.

                    GIRL
          It's a hard lot to park in.

Birgitta looks toward the glass. The lot outside is empty except for her own car, rain bouncing off the hood.

                    BIRGITTA
          What's your name, sweetheart?

                    GIRL
          Noor.

                    BIRGITTA
          Noor, I'm going to call someone to help us find your dad. Okay?

Noor's face changes. Her shoulders come up. She grabs her backpack straps in both fists.

                    NOOR
          Don't. He'll be mad. I'm not supposed to tell people where we live.

                    BIRGITTA
          I don't need to know where you live.

                    NOOR
          Then who are you calling?

The front door BANGS open. A MAN (40s, wet work jacket, breathing hard) stands in the doorway scanning the room. He sees Noor. Everything in his posture collapses at once. Tobias, startled, drops the gummies. Birgitta rises in front of both children.

                    MAN
          Noor.

                    NOOR
          Dad.

She runs to him. He drops to his knees and wraps both arms around her. Birgitta watches the two of them, her hand on Tobias's head.

                    BIRGITTA
          She's been here an hour.

                    MAN
          The truck wouldn't start. I ran all the way from the bridge. Thank you. Thank you for --

                    BIRGITTA
          Her name is Noor?

                    MAN
          Noor. Yes.

He kisses the top of her head. Lifts her. She buries her face in his collar. Tobias picks up his gummies and Gary and trots back to his mother.

                    TOBIAS
          Mom? Gary says goodbye.

Noor lifts her head from her father's shoulder and, very seriously, waves.
